{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9d7b57f074bdd1c7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Component: kserve\n2\t\n3\t## Metadata\n4\t\n5\t- **Repository**: https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/kserve.git\n6\t- **Version**: da0e07f0d03c7f0c9c7fa0232d5c51d068e64452\n7\t- **Distribution**: RHOAI\n8\t- **Languages**: Go, Python\n9\t- **Deployment Type**: Kubernetes Operator / Controller + Python SDK + Sidecar utilities\n10\t- **Generated By**: Claude Opus 4.6\n11\t\n12\t## Purpose\n13\t\n14\t**Short**: KServe is a Kubernetes-native model serving platform that manages the lifecycle of inference services, LLM serving deployments, inference graphs, and local model caching through multiple controller binaries, admission webhooks, and a Python SDK. [source: Dockerfile:50, Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux.kserve-module-controller:33, config/webhook/manifests.yaml:2, pkg/controller/v1beta1/inferenceservice/controller.go:724]\n15\t\n16\t**Detailed**: KServe provides the core model serving infrastructure for RHOAI, managing inference workloads from single-model deployments through multi-model inference graphs and LLM-optimized serving. The platform is composed of four main controller binaries — the core kserve manager (InferenceService, InferenceGraph, TrainedModel), the LLMInferenceService controller (llmisvc), the local model caching controllers (localmodel/localmodelnode), and the kserve-module operator that manages component installation on OpenShift. Each controller reconciles its CRDs and owns downstream Kubernetes resources (Deployments, Services, HTTPRoutes, PersistentVolumes) through controller-runtime watches. The repository also ships a Python SDK (`kserve` package) with framework-specific model servers (HuggingFace, sklearn, XGBoost, LightGBM, vLLM, and others) that implement the KServe V2 Inference Protocol via both HTTP (FastAPI/uvicorn) and gRPC endpoints. An inference router binary provides authenticated request routing for InferenceGraph DAGs, enforcing access control via Kubernetes TokenReview and SubjectAccessReview APIs. [source: cmd/manager/main.go:288, cmd/llmisvc/main.go:339, cmd/router/main.go:525, kserve-module/cmd/kserve-module/main.go:91, python/kserve/kserve/protocol/grpc/grpc_predict_v2.proto:23]\n17\t\n18\t## Architectural Analysis\n19\t\n20\tKServe follows a multi-controller operator architecture with clear separation between the upstream KServe controllers and the RHOAI-specific kserve-module operator. The core repository ships four independent controller binaries, each running as separate processes with dedicated RBAC identities and leader election.\n21\t\n22\t**Controller topology.** The `manager` binary reconciles the foundational CRDs — InferenceService, InferenceGraph, ClusterServingRuntime, and TrainedModel — creating Deployments, Services, and ingress resources (HTTPRoutes, Istio VirtualServices, or OpenShift Routes depending on platform configuration). The `llmisvc` controller handles the LLMInferenceService and LLMInferenceServiceConfig CRDs with extensive Gateway API integration, managing HorizontalPodAutoscalers, LeaderWorkerSets for multi-node inference, KEDA ScaledObjects, and InferencePool resources for gateway-based load balancing. The `localmodel`/`localmodelnode` pair manages persistent model caching at cluster and node level through PersistentVolume/PersistentVolumeClaim lifecycle and batch Job-based model downloads. The `kserve-module` operator runs from a separate Go module (`kserve-module/`) and manages the installation and configuration of all other KServe components on the ODH/RHOAI platform, watching the `Kserve` CR from `components.platform.opendatahub.io`. [source: pkg/controller/v1beta1/inferenceservice/controller.go:724, pkg/controller/v1alpha2/llmisvc/controller.go:419, pkg/controller/v1alpha1/localmodel/reconcilers/localmodelcache_reconciler.go:365, kserve-module/pkg/kservemodule/setup.go:68]\n23\t\n24\t**Authentication and authorization.** The router binary implements a full Kubernetes-native authentication chain for InferenceGraph requests: it extracts Bearer tokens from the Authorization header, validates them via the Kubernetes TokenReview API, and then checks authorization via SubjectAccessReview against the specific InferenceGraph resource in the pod's namespace. This ensures that only users with `get` permission on the target InferenceGraph can route inference requests. Health and readiness probes across all controllers are intentionally unauthenticated. The controller manager's metrics endpoint is protected by a kube-rbac-proxy sidecar that terminates TLS on port 8443 and proxies authenticated requests to the manager on localhost:8080 — though the ODH overlay removes this sidecar and replaces it with odh-kube-auth-proxy. [source: cmd/router/main.go:525-614, config/default/manager_auth_proxy_patch.yaml:22, config/overlays/odh/patches/remove-auth-proxy-patch.yaml:10]\n25\t\n26\t**TLS and platform integration.** The ODH/RHOAI distribution build (`//go:build distro`) includes platform-aware TLS configuration that reads the cluster's TLS security profile from `apiservers.config.openshift.io/cluster` and applies the corresponding cipher suites and minimum TLS version. When the profile is unavailable (non-OpenShift or transient failure), it falls back to Mozilla Intermediate cipher suites with TLS 1.2 minimum. This allows the platform to enforce consistent TLS policy across all KServe controller endpoints without per-component configuration. [source: pkg/tls/tls_odh.go:73-100]\n27\t\n28\t**FIPS build posture.** The Konflux-built kserve-module-controller image uses `CGO_ENABLED=1`, `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime`, and `-tags strictfipsruntime`, enabling FIPS-validated cryptography through the Go runtime's OpenSSL backend. The upstream Dockerfile for the core manager uses `CGO_ENABLED=0` (static linking), which would not pass the check-payload FIPS gate; the Konflux build pipeline for this binary is expected to override these flags on the downstream release branch. [source: Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux.kserve-module-controller:21, Dockerfile:21]\n29\t\n30\t**Internal platform dependencies.** The kserve-module operator depends on `odh-platform-utilities` for platform detection, manifest rendering, and deployment helpers. The LLMInferenceService controller integrates with the Gateway API Inference Extension (`sigs.k8s.io/gateway-api-inference-extension`) for InferencePool type conversion, enabling gateway-based routing of inference traffic. Gateway API watches are conditional — the controller checks for CRD availability before registering watches — allowing graceful degradation when Gateway API is not installed. cert-manager integration provides automated TLS certificate management through Certificate and ClusterIssuer CRDs. [source: kserve-module/pkg/apis/v1alpha1/types.go:9, pkg/apis/gie/v1alpha2pool/inferencepool_conversion.go:29, pkg/controller/v1alpha2/llmisvc/controller.go:444]\n31\t\n32\t## Provenance\n33\t\n34\t### Repo Lineage\n35\t\n36\t| Role | Repository | Sync Mechanism | Sync Branch | Sync Workflows | Detection Method |\n37\t|----|----------|--------------|-----------|--------------|----------------|\n38\t| Upstream | https://github.com/kserve/kserve | -- | -- | -- | known_mapping |\n39\t| Midstream | https://github.com/opendatahub-io/kserve | auto_merge | release-v0.17 | -- | sync_config |\n40\t| Downstream | https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/kserve | auto_merge | release-v0.17 | `sync-master-to-release.yaml` | local_analysis |\n41\t\n42\t### Aliases\n43\t\n44\t| Current Name | Previous Name | Type | Context |\n45\t|------------|-------------|----|-------|\n46\t\n47\t## Architecture Components\n48\t\n49\t| Component | Type | Purpose |\n50\t|---------|----|-------|\n51\t| Dockerfile:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/manager\"] |\n52\t| Dockerfiles/Dockerfile.konflux.kserve-module-controller:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | 