ion context?\" — before the standalone KEDA implementation work starts. Produce an analysis that enumerates every downstream adaptation the upstream PR implies for the RHOAI kserve fork (API/CRD surface, CEL validation, controller reconciliation, RBAC, TLS, webhook and manifest changes) and recommends the integration strategy (consume the upstream merge vs. carry the patch downstream with backport).\n\n## Scope\n\n- Analyze the current revision of upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 (standalone KEDA support for LLMInferenceService, per accepted design kserve/kserve#5646) against the RHOAI kserve fork (midstream opendatahub-io/kserve and downstream red-hat-data-services/kserve).\n- Enumerate downstream adaptation items beyond the known odh-model-controller TriggerAuthentication flow, with emphasis on potential RBAC and TLS adjustments for OpenShift called out in the strategy.\n- Classify each item as: already covered by planned implementation scope, or additional work requiring scope change.\n- Recommend the integration approach: wait for upstream merge, or carry the patch downstream (the strategy's stated fallback), including any backport considerations.\n- Identify any risks to the mutual-exclusivity CEL validation design, the actuator-consistency constraint for disaggregated deployments (architecture overlay 0011), and scaling status conditions.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- An analysis document exists that enumerates, item by item, the downstream adaptation kserve/kserve#5839 requires beyond the odh-model-controller authentication context, each classified as covered or additional.\n- The integration recommendation (upstream merge vs. patch carry, with backport notes) is stated with rationale grounded in the PR's status.\n- Potential RBAC and TLS adjustments for OpenShift are explicitly assessed (needed or not, and where they would land).\n- The findings are reviewed with the Serving team and the outcome is recorded so the standalone KEDA implementation scope can be finalized.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\nNo HLR is directly implemented by this epic. It resolves the open question gating the scope of the standalone KEDA implementation (HLR-1 through HLR-6); its outcome determines whether the implementation epic's scope needs rewriting.\n```\n\n**E002 body:**\n\n```markdown\n# Standalone KEDA autoscaling path for LLMInferenceService in the llmisvc-controller\n\n## Description\n\nDeliver standalone KEDA autoscaling as a first-class path for LLMInferenceService in the RHOAI kserve llmisvc-controller, alongside the existing WVA-mediated path, following the upstream design kserve/kserve#5646 and implementation PR kserve/kserve#5839. Users configure KEDA triggers directly in the LLMInferenceService spec without deploying WVA, reaching feature parity with v1beta1 InferenceService. Covers the API surface, CEL validation, controller reconciliation branching, and scaling status conditions for both paths, including scale-to-zero, scale-from-zero, and disaggregated prefill/decode scaling.\n\n## Scope\n\n- API type changes in `pkg/apis/serving/v1alpha2/llm_inference_service_types.go`: a standalone KEDA spec at the ScalingSpec level with `pollingInterval`, `cooldownPeriod`, and a `triggers` field for user-defined KEDA trigger definitions (type, metadata map).\n- CEL validation changes in `pkg/apis/serving/v1alpha2/llm_inference_service_validation.go`: relax the rule requiring `wva` to requiring at least one of `wva` or `keda` when `spec.scaling` is configured; enforce at-most-one (mutual exclusivity), mutual exclusivity with static `spec.replicas`, and trigger completeness. For disaggregated deployments, both prefill and decode phases must use the same scaling path (actuator consistency, architecture overlay 0011).\n- Controller changes in `pkg/controller/v1alpha2/llmisvc/scaling.go`: branch on the configured path — standalone KEDA creates a ScaledObject from user-provided triggers (Resource, External, PodMetric types per the v1beta1 reference reconciler at `pkg/controller/v1beta1/inferenceservice/reconcilers/keda/keda_reconciler.go`) and skips VariantAutoscaling CR creation entirely.\n- Scale-to-zero via KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` for both paths; scale-from-zero in the standalone path driven by user-configured triggers; independent per-phase scaling for disaggregated deployments via phase-specific ScaledObjects.\n- Scaling status conditions and scaling-decision events for both paths (KEDA ScaledObject events for the standalone path; WVA events for the WVA path) so operators see scaling events with signal attribution.\n- Backwards compatibility: static deployments with no autoscaling behave identically; existing CRs with `spec.scaling.wva` continue unchanged.\n- Integration tests across standalone KEDA x 2 topologies (integrated and disaggregated).\n- Upstream alignment: consume kserve/kserve#5839 once merged; if the PR is delayed, carry the patch downstream with backport (the strategy's stated fallback).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. Given an LLMInferenceService with `spec.scaling.keda` configured with user-defined triggers and no WVA deployed, when a trigger condition is met (e.g., Prometheus query threshold exceeded), then the deployment scales up — measured by KEDA ScaledObject status showing active and Deployment replica count increasing.\n2. Given an LLMInferenceService with both `spec.scaling.wva` and `spec.scaling.keda` set, when the CR is submitted, then the API server rejects it with a CEL validation error indicating mutual exclusivity; a `spec.scaling` with neither path is likewise rejected (at-least-one).\n3. Given an LLMInferenceService with `spec.scaling.keda` and no `spec.scaling.wva`, when the llmisvc-controller reconciles, then no VariantAutoscaling CR is created and a ScaledObject with the user-provided triggers is created — measured by `kubectl get variantautoscalings` returning empty and `kubectl get scaledobjects` returning the ScaledObject with the user's triggers.\n4. Given an LLMInferenceService with KEDA and `idleReplicaCount: 0` (either path), when no requests are received for the cooldown period, then the deployment scales to zero — measured by Deployment replica count reaching 0 and KEDA ScaledObject status showing idle; a subsequent trigger firing scales it back up to serving (scale-from-zero).\n5. Given a disaggregated LLMInferenceService with separate scaling configs (either path), when prefill pods reach saturation while decode pods remain below threshold, then only the prefill pool scales up — measured by independent scaling resource status for prefill and decode roles; validation rejects mixed scaling paths across phases.\n6. Given a deployment with autoscaling active (either path), when Prometheus metrics become unavailable, then the deployment holds its current replica count — measured by replica count remaining stable during the metric outage.\n7. Static deployments with no autoscaling configured continue to serve identically — measured by no VariantAutoscaling, HPA, or ScaledObject resources created for deployments without `spec.scaling`; existing CRs with `spec.scaling.wva` produce identical resources as before the change.\n8. Scaling events are visible to operators with signal attribution for both paths — measured by KEDA ScaledObject events for the standalone path and WVA events for the WVA path, and scaling status conditions reflecting the active path.\n9. Integration tests pass for standalone KEDA across both topologies (integrated and disaggregated), including scale-to-zero and scale-from-zero cycles.\n10. The delivered changes are consistent with kserve/kserve#5839 (consumed from upstream), or the patch is carried downstream with a backport plan if the upstream PR is delayed.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 [P0]: LLMInferenceService supports standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without requiring WVA deployment.\n- HLR-2 [P0]: The two autoscaling paths are mutually exclusive at the API level with CEL validation enforcing at-least-one and at-most-one.\n- HLR-3 [P0]: Scale-to-zero via KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` (both paths).\n- HLR-4 [P0]: Scale-from-zero (standalone KEDA via user-configured triggers).\n- HLR-5 [P0]: Disaggregated prefill and decode pools scale independently.\n- HLR-6 [P1]: Scaling events visible to operators with signal attribution (both paths). Bundled with the P0 work deliberately: it shares the same controller and status-condition surface — splitting would build the same surface twice.\n```\n\n**E003 body:**\n\n```markdown\n# KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA Prometheus triggers\n\n## Description\n\nProvide the downstream authentication context that KEDA Prometheus-type triggers need on OpenShift for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA autoscaling: a parallel reconciler flow in odh-model-controller that creates the per-namespace authentication resources (dedicated ServiceAccount, ServiceAccountToken Secret, Role/RoleBinding for metrics access, and a KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR supplying bearer token and CA cert), following the existing `kserve_keda_reconciler` pattern used for v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA autoscaling.\n\n## Scope\n\n- New reconciler flow in odh-model-controller (alongside `internal/controller/serving/reconcilers/kserve_keda_reconciler.go`) that reacts to LLMInferenceService resources configured for standalone KEDA with Prometheus-type triggers.\n- Create per-namespace authentication resources: ServiceAccount (`inference-prometheus-auth` pattern), ServiceAccountToken Secret (`kubernetes.io/service-account-token` type), Role/RoleBinding for metrics access, and the KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR.\n- Reuse the existing reconciler's conventions for resource naming, owner reference management, and lifecycle cleanup so resources are garbage-collected when the LLMInferenceService KEDA configuration is removed.\n- No changes to the existing v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA authentication flow (no regression).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. Given an LLMInferenceService with standalone KEDA Prometheus-type triggers, when odh-model-controller reconciles, then the per-namespace authentication context exists — ServiceAccount, ServiceAccountToken Secret, Role/RoleBinding, and TriggerAuthentication CR — owned by the LLMInferenceService.\n2. Given a KEDA Prometheus trigger that references the TriggerAuthentication, when KEDA queries Thanos/Prometheus, then the query succeeds with the ServiceAccount bearer token over TLS 1.2+ (authenticated query returns metrics).\n3. Given an LLMInferenceService whose standalone KEDA configuration is removed, when reconciliation completes, then the authentication resources are cleaned up via owner reference lifecycle.\n4. Existing v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA authentication behavior is unchanged — measured by the existing reconciler's tests passing and no resource changes for v1beta1 InferenceServices.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 [P0] (OpenShift enablement): standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers — the primary supported trigger source (Prometheus/Thanos) requires this authentication context on OpenShift.\n```\n\n**E004 body:**\n\n```markdown\n# LLMInferenceService autoscaling documentation: decision guide, recipes, troubleshooting\n\n## Description\n\nAuthor the well-lit path documentation for LLMInferenceService autoscaling covering both paths — WVA-mediated autoscaling and standalone KEDA — plus static allocation: a decision guide, configuration recipes per workload shape, troubleshooting, and a supported configuration matrix. Includes KEDA prerequisite guidance, scale-from-zero trigger recommendations using EPP flow control queue metrics, and TriggerAuthentication reference guidance for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Decision guide: WVA-mediated autoscaling vs. standalone KEDA vs. static allocation, with use-case-based recommendations (e.g., the coding-assistant deployment that scales prefill and decode pools during business hours and releases GPUs overnight).\n- Configuration recipes per workload shape, covering both paths, scale-to-zero (`idleReplicaCount: 0`) and scale-from-zero; recommended scale-from-zero trigger configurations using EPP flow control queue metrics, with example ScaledObject templates.\n- Guidance to reference the KEDA TriggerAuthentication resource in trigger definitions for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift.\n- KEDA as a documented prerequisite with installation guidance referencing the GitOps repository (ODH-ADR-0007; RHOAI does not auto-install external operators — platform policy overlay 0008).\n- Supported configuration matrix and troubleshooting paths, including misconfigured scale-from-zero triggers (requests waiting indefinitely) and behavior during Prometheus metric outages.\n- Scale-from-zero latency expectations and headroom pre-scaling recommendations for latency-sensitive workloads, incorporating the target p50/p95 agreed with the llm-d team (the RFE references a 60-second p50 threshold), referencing the fast model startup RFEs (RHAIRFE-7753, RHAIRFE-7599, RHAIRFE-7139).\n- Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet-scale deployments (KEDA polling intervals at scale), incorporating Performance/PSAP team input.\n- Content Outline (inside Scope per artifact contract): the above topics constitute the outline; each becomes a documented topic in the RHOAI documentation set.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. The decision guide covers all three allocation approaches (WVA-mediated, standalone KEDA, static) with use-case-based recommendations.\n2. Configuration recipes exist for both autoscaling paths across representative workload shapes, including disaggregated prefill/decode deployments.\n3. Example ScaledObject templates for scale-from-zero using EPP flow control queue metrics are provided and validated against the implemented behavior (including metric availability when pods are at zero replicas and EPP remains running).\n4. Documentation instructs operators to reference the TriggerAuthentication resource for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift.\n5. KEDA installation is documented as a prerequisite referencing the GitOps repository.\n6. The supported configuration matrix and troubleshooting section cover invalid scaling-path combinations (CEL mutual exclusivity errors), misconfigured scale-from-zero triggers, and metric-outage behavior.\n7. Scale-from-zero latency expectations and headroom recommendations are documented, incorporating the p50/p95 target from the llm-d team.\n8. Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet-scale deployments is included, incorporating Performance/PSAP input.\n9. All content is technically reviewed against the implemented autoscaling paths (engineering review of the documented behavior, commands, and example manifests).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-7 [P1]: Well-lit path documentation covers both autoscaling paths with decision guide, configuration recipes per workload shape, and troubleshooting.\n- HLR-4 [P0] (guidance aspect): scale-from-zero via user-configured triggers — recommended trigger configurations and example templates.\n```\n\nHmm wait — in E004 Scope I wrote \"Content Outline (inside Scope per artifact contract)\" — that's meta-commentary; better to remove that line. The scope list IS the outline. Remove it.\n\nAlso E004's dependencies must include both E002 and E003 (Rule 11). Body shouldn't mention \"E002/E003\" — it says \"the implemented autoscaling paths\" — fine. Description says \"both paths\" — fine.\n\nOne check on E002 body: \"alongside the existing WVA-mediated path\" — fine, no sibling references. E001 body: no sibling epic references — I mention \"the standalone KEDA implementation\" generically. Good. E003: no sibling refs — mentions LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA API generally. Good.\n\nNow signal files. One per epic: RHAISTRAT-8008-E001-ai-signals.md (investigation s