 path\"]\n    E002 --> E003[\"RHAISTRAT-8008-E003 odh-model-controller TriggerAuthentication\"]\n    E002 --> E004[\"RHAISTRAT-8008-E004 autoscaling documentation\"]\n    E003 --> E004\n```\n\nArrows point from dependency to dependent (E001 must complete before E002 can start).\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation edges) | The strategy explicitly flags the open question \"Does the upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 require downstream adaptation beyond authentication context?\" The answer changes the scope/approach of the kserve integration (clean consume/backport vs. additional RBAC/TLS/webhook adaptation work), so it is a true gate with `rewrite` impact, recorded via `gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact` on E002. |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The odh-model-controller reconciler code builds against the LLMInferenceService v1alpha2 API types (the new `spec.scaling.keda` field) produced by the kserve work — odh-model-controller imports the kserve Go API module (architecture context: odh-model-controller.md, `github.com/kserve/kserve` dependency). A CRD/API artifact, not a configuration reference, so the edge is serial. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built; the docs-authoring epic is blocked by every Implementation epic in the strategy. |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | The TriggerAuthentication flow must exist for the documentation to instruct operators to reference it in KEDA trigger definitions. |\n\nNote: E001's gate on E003 is transitive via E002 (E003 cannot start before E002 completes, which cannot start before E001 resolves) — no separate redundant edge is added.\n\n## Component Mapping (canonical names from .context/rhai-components.txt)\n\n- \"KServe (llmisvc-controller)\" → `Serving Orchestration` — there is no canonical \"KServe\" component; the llmisvc-controller is the serving control-plane orchestrator for LLMInferenceService, which is the closest canonical bucket.\n- \"odh-model-controller\" → `Serving Orchestration` — same serving control-plane subsystem; kept as a separate epic because it is a distinct repo and logical change.\n- \"Documentation\" → `Documentation` — verbatim match.\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n|-----|----------|---------|\n| HLR-1: LLMInferenceService supports standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without requiring WVA deployment | P0 | E002, E003 |\n| HLR-2: Two autoscaling paths mutually exclusive at API level; CEL validation enforcing at-least-one and at-most-one | P0 | E002 |\n| HLR-3: Scale-to-zero via KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` (both paths) | P0 | E002 |\n| HLR-4: Scale-from-zero (WVA via EPP queue metrics; standalone KEDA via user-configured triggers) | P0 | E002, E004 (trigger guidance) |\n| HLR-5: Disaggregated prefill and decode pools scale independently | P0 | E002 |\n| HLR-6: Scaling events visible to operators with signal attribution | P1 | E002 |\n| HLR-7: Well-lit path documentation with decision guide, recipes, troubleshooting | P1 | E004 |\n\nFull coverage; no orphaned HLRs.\n\n### Priority decisions\n\n- P1 HLR-6 (scaling events / signal attribution) stays bundled in E002: it shares the same controller, status-condition, and event surface as the P0 work — it is not a distinct, deferrable feature with independent surfaces, and splitting it would make two epics build the same status surface.\n- E004 (docs-authoring) is P0 via the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` → P0), not via priority inheritance; per Rule 11 its edges do not push priority upstream.\n- E001 is P0 by priority inheritance: it blocks all P0 implementation work.\n\n## Unknowns evaluated and NOT promoted to Investigation epics\n\n- \"KEDA ScaledObject with `idleReplicaCount: 0` correctly pauses and resumes\" (assumption, needs validation): the implementation approach is fixed by the accepted upstream design; failure would be fix-and-retry, not a scope change → acceptance criteria on E002 (scale-to-zero / scale-from-zero integration tests).\n- \"EPP flow control queue metrics reliably available when pods are at zero replicas\" (assumption, needs validation): the answer changes documentation recommendations, not which epics exist or what the controller implements → scope and acceptance criteria on E004 (validated example trigger configurations).\n- \"Target p50/p95 for scale-from-zero time\" (open question, llm-d team): an input to documentation content (headroom pre-scaling recommendations), not to epic structure → E004 scope item and stakeholder input.\n- \"Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet-scale deployments\" (open question, Performance/PSAP team): documentation content input → E004 scope item and stakeholder input.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nEvaluated per Rule 21 / Step 5.5: the only external dependency with uncertain timing is upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 (open, assigned, linked to accepted design kserve/kserve#5646). A viable fallback exists — RHOAI carries the patch downstream with backport once merged — so the dependency is modeled as an acceptance criterion and risk on E002 rather than a separate upstream epic. Partial-value tiering is not applicable: the standalone KEDA path is not deliverable in a meaningful reduced form without the kserve API/controller changes, and the fallback removes the need to split Tier 1/Tier 2. No tiered delivery split.\n\n## Health Warnings (non-blocking)\n\n1. **Upstream timing risk**: kserve/kserve#5839 is in progress; if it stalls, the kserve integration timeline slips unless the patch-carry fallback is exercised. Captured as an acceptance criterion and risk on E002.\n2. **External content inputs to documentation**: E004 requires inputs from teams outside this strategy — the llm-d team (scale-from-zero p50/p95 target) and Performance/PSAP (Prometheus capacity guidance). These are stakeholder commitments the docs team does not control; a human should secure them early.\n3. **Proposed acceptance criteria / pending process gates**: the strategy marks its ACs as \"Proposed — requires PM/Engineering validation\", and all Prerequisites & Process Gates (architecture review, build onboarding, license validation, etc.) are \"Pending human review\". The decomposition proceeds on the proposed ACs; that human validation remains outstanding.\n4. **No RHOAI-specific scalability target**: the NFR section flags that no target exists for concurrent ScaledObjects; capacity guidance is deferred to documentation (E004) — a potential scope trap if a hard target is later imposed.\n\n## Scope Notes\n\n- Integration testing across standalone KEDA x 2 topologies (integrated and disaggregated) is folded into E002/E003 acceptance criteria rather than a separate epic: the strategy's Impacted Teams table lists no separate QE team, and testing has no separate component boundary here.\n- The strategy is additive (standalone KEDA alongside WVA), not a capability replacement, so no feature-flag/rollback epic is generated (Rule 23); backwards compatibility for static deployments and existing `spec.scaling.wva` CRs is enforced as E002 acceptance criteria, with CEL mutual-exclusivity validation as the guard.\n- No onboarding chain is generated (Rule 20): kserve and odh-model-controller both exist in the platform (architecture context files present), and Documentation needs no repo onboarding.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":94366,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f4cc6a4428f1cc4","agent_id":"aa54ef1391fcef3a7","session_id":"207785e0-7a4d-4b13-b5f6-b50ef683c695","uuid":"e83c5655-0ad9-4812-b18b-2d794fe6448b","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:51:55.296Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8008"}
