rted configuration matrix and troubleshooting paths, including misconfigured scale-from-zero triggers (requests waiting indefinitely at the EPP) and behavior during Prometheus metric outages.\n- Scale-from-zero latency expectations and headroom pre-scaling recommendations for latency-sensitive workloads, incorporating the target p50/p95 to be confirmed with the llm-d team (the RFE references a 60-second p50 threshold), and referencing the fast model startup RFEs (RHAIRFE-7753, RHAIRFE-7599, RHAIRFE-7139).\n- Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet-scale deployments (KEDA polling intervals at scale), incorporating Performance/PSAP team input.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n1. The decision guide covers all three allocation approaches (WVA-mediated, standalone KEDA, static) with use-case-based recommendations for choosing between them.\n2. Configuration recipes exist for both autoscaling paths across representative workload shapes, including disaggregated prefill/decode deployments.\n3. Example ScaledObject templates for scale-from-zero using EPP flow control queue metrics are provided and validated against the implemented behavior, including metric availability when pods are at zero replicas and EPP remains running.\n4. Documentation instructs operators to reference the TriggerAuthentication resource in KEDA trigger definitions for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift.\n5. KEDA installation is documented as a prerequisite, referencing the GitOps repository.\n6. The supported configuration matrix and troubleshooting section cover invalid scaling-path combinations (CEL mutual-exclusivity errors), misconfigured scale-from-zero triggers, and metric-outage behavior.\n7. Scale-from-zero latency expectations and headroom pre-scaling recommendations are documented, incorporating the p50/p95 target confirmed with the llm-d team.\n8. Prometheus capacity guidance for fleet-scale deployments is included, incorporating Performance/PSAP team input.\n9. All content is technically reviewed against the implemented autoscaling paths — engineering review of the documented behavior, commands, and example manifests.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-7 [P1]: Well-lit path documentation covers both autoscaling paths with decision guide, configuration recipes per workload shape, and troubleshooting.\n- HLR-4 [P0] (guidance aspect): Scale-from-zero via user-configured triggers — recommended trigger configurations and example ScaledObject templates.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":100076,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9f4cc6a4428f1cc4","agent_id":"aa54ef1391fcef3a7","session_id":"207785e0-7a4d-4b13-b5f6-b50ef683c695","uuid":"248f7ea6-0c2e-42a8-9621-d031873e521e","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:53:00.251Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8008"}
