{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc001a2d04c93941","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8008-E001\n3\ttitle: Downstream adaptation analysis for upstream standalone KEDA support (kserve/kserve#5839)\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8008\n5\tcomponent: Serving Orchestration\n6\tteam: Serving team\n7\ttype: Investigation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies: []\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 1\n13\t  local_runnability: 0\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: -1\n15\t  human_judgment_required: 0\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tai_signals: null\n18\tai_implementability: Medium\n19\tai_implementability_score: 1\n20\tjira_key: null\n21\tbranch: null\n22\tgated_by: null\n23\tgate_failure_impact: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Downstream adaptation analysis for upstream standalone KEDA support (kserve/kserve#5839)\n26\t\n27\t## Description\n28\t\n29\tResolve the strategy's open question — \"Does the upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 require downstream adaptation beyond the authentication context?\" — before the standalone KEDA implementation work starts. Produce an analysis that enumerates every downstream adaptation the upstream PR implies for the RHOAI kserve fork (API/CRD surface, CEL validation, controller reconciliation, RBAC, TLS, webhooks, manifests) and recommends the integration strategy: consume the upstream merge, or carry the patch downstream with backport.\n30\t\n31\t## Scope\n32\t\n33\t- Analyze the current revision of upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 (standalone KEDA support for LLMInferenceService, implementing accepted design kserve/kserve#5646) against the RHOAI kserve fork (midstream opendatahub-io/kserve and downstream red-hat-data-services/kserve).\n34\t- Enumerate downstream adaptation items beyond the known odh-model-controller TriggerAuthentication flow, with emphasis on the potential RBAC and TLS adjustments for OpenShift called out in the strategy.\n35\t- Classify each item as already covered by the planned implementation scope, or additional work requiring a scope change.\n36\t- Recommend the integration approach — wait for upstream merge vs. carry the patch downstream (the strategy's stated fallback) — including backport considerations.\n37\t- Assess risks to the mutual-exclusivity CEL validation design, the actuator-consistency constraint for disaggregated deployments (architecture overlay 0011), and the scaling status conditions for both paths.\n38\t\n39\t## Acceptance Criteria\n40\t\n41\t1. An analysis document exists that enumerates, item by item, the downstream adaptation kserve/kserve#5839 requires beyond the odh-model-controller authentication context, with each item classified as covered or additional.\n42\t2. The integration recommendation (upstream merge vs. patch carry, with backport notes) is stated with rationale grounded in the PR's current status.\n43\t3. Potential RBAC and TLS adjustments for OpenShift are explicitly assessed — needed or not, and where each would land.\n44\t4. The findings are reviewed with the Serving team and the outcome recorded, so the standalone KEDA implementation scope can be finalized from it.\n45\t\n46\t## HLR Traceability\n47\t\n48\tNo HLR is directly implemented by this epic. It resolves the strategy open question that gates the scope of the standalone KEDA implementation covering HLR-1 through HLR-6; its outcome determines whether that implementation scope needs rewriting.\n49\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8ec4cd4f3ce983d6","agent_id":"a91d5948a2ac01e55","session_id":"207785e0-7a4d-4b13-b5f6-b50ef683c695","uuid":"df753ea4-6bac-4872-baaf-6fab37691aed","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:55:10.190Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc001a2d04c93941","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8008 decomposition"}
