controller/status-condition/event surface as the P0 work, so it is not an independently deferrable feature (no priority collapse).\n34\t\n35\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n36\tNo cycles; every edge in the summary diagram matches the target epic's frontmatter `dependencies` exactly (E001→E002, E002→E003, E002→E004, E003→E004) and vice versa; arrows point dependency→dependent per convention. Critical path length 4 fits the M-standard heuristic (3-4). Edge justifications check out: E001→E002 is a true Investigation gate (`gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact` with rewrite impact, matching the strategy's open question on kserve/kserve#5839 downstream adaptation); E002→E003 is a Rule 13 build-against artifact edge — odh-model-controller compiles against the kserve Go API module and the new `spec.scaling.keda` types, a code/API artifact, not an image-digest/endpoint configuration reference, so a serial edge (not an AC gate) is correct; E002→E004 and E003→E004 are Rule 11 docs-last edges and are not priority-inheritance violations. No konflux-onboarding/license-validation/repo-onboarding epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are not applicable; E002 and E003 are correctly left un-coupled except for the genuine API-artifact edge. No branch files exist and none are needed (the gate outcome is handled via `gate_failure_impact`, not divergent epic sets). No data/artifact dependency discoverable from epic content is missing from the DAG — E003's reconciler consumes E002's API surface (edge present) and E004's TriggerAuthentication guidance consumes E003 (edge present).\n37\t\n38\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n39\tEach epic is a distinct logical change: E001 (analysis), E002 (kserve llmisvc-controller repo), E003 (odh-model-controller repo), E004 (Documentation team, docs-authoring). No epic bundles multiple teams; E002 and E003 share the `Serving Orchestration` component and team but are correctly split as separate repos/logical changes rather than merged. No scope duplication: E002 creates ScaledObjects in the kserve controller, E003 creates the per-namespace auth context in odh-model-controller — disjoint surfaces. No work-product type mixing (code vs. content authoring are in separate epics). E002 sits at the upper bound of the size guideline (the strategy estimates 1-2 sprints for the kserve API/controller work) — noted as a minor, but it is one logical change in one controller with an upstream reference implementation, so splitting it would create an artificial boundary.\n40\t\n41\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n42\tE001 is a genuine Investigation: its outcome (clean consume vs. patch-carry plus possible RBAC/TLS/webhook adaptation) changes E002's scope, and E002 exists downstream of it. E002 correctly carries `gated_by: RHAISTRAT-8008-E001` with `gate_failure_impact` populating both `action` (rewrite) and `fallback_approach`, and the `gated_by` target is in E002's direct `dependencies`. E002/E003/E004 are Implementations producing concrete artifacts (controller code + tests, reconciler code + tests, published documentation). E003 and E004 list no Investigation in `dependencies`, so no missing `gated_by`. E004's `implementation_type: docs-authoring` is set correctly. No misclassifications.\n43\t\n44\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n45\tEach epic carries the rubric matching its type: E001 has `investigation_signals` (5-signal) with `ai_signals: null`; E002/E003/E004 have `ai_signals` (9-signal) with `investigation_signals: null`. Signal values are consistent with rationales and strategy content: E001 `source_accessibility: 1` is justified (open GitHub PR/repos, not a pending human decision); E002 `open_questions: -1` and `external_dependency: -1` correctly reflect the unresolved upstream gate and kserve/kserve#5839 timing; E003 `open_questions: 0` is consistent with the resolved, pattern-replicating design; E004 `open_questions: -1` and `human_process_gates: -1` match the unresolved stakeholder content inputs and the mandatory technical review. Rationale files exist for all four epics and match the frontmatter values. (Arithmetic/thresholds not checked per instructions.)\n46\t\n47\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n48\tAll four epics have testable ACs derived from the strategy — E002's ten ACs cover all seven proposed strategy ACs (scale-up on trigger, mutual exclusivity CEL errors both directions, no VariantAutoscaling + ScaledObject created, scale-to/from-zero, per-phase disaggregated scaling, metric-outage stability, static-deployment backwards compatibility) plus events attribution and integration tests across both topologies. Rule-mandated ACs present where applicable: E004 AC 9 is the docs technical-review AC; no konflux chain or replacement (feature-flag/rollback) rules apply — the change is additive and the decomposition's Scope Notes correctly justify this against Rule 23. No draft-ID (E00x) references appear in any epic body. Minor: E004 ACs 7 and 8 hinge on external stakeholder confirmations (llm-d p50/p95 target, Performance/PSAP capacity guidance) that the docs team does not control — the dependency is properly surfaced in the summary's Health Warnings, but the ACs as written are only satisfiable if those inputs arrive; a fallback (\"documented with the currently known 60-second p50 reference and TBD markers\") would make them self-contained.\n49\t\n50\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n51\tAll strategy scope is accounted for: API/validation/controller/status-conditions work (E002), TriggerAuthentication downstream flow (E003), and every documentation deliverable the strategy names — decision guide over all three allocation approaches, per-workload-shape recipes, troubleshooting, supported configuration matrix, KEDA prerequisite guidance (ODH-ADR-0007 / overlay 0008), scale-from-zero trigger templates, latency expectations, and fleet-scale Prometheus capacity guidance (E004). NFRs carried: backwards compatibility (E002 AC 7), observability (E002 AC 8 + E004), security (E003 AC 2), scalability (E004 + health warning). All three strategy open questions are dispositioned (Q1 → E001 investigation; Q2, Q3 → E004 scope), and the remaining assumptions are correctly evaluated as ACs/scope rather than promoted to investigations. Risks are carried into epic scope/ACs (upstream timing → E002 AC 10; misconfigured triggers → E004 troubleshooting). Out-of-scope boundaries respected (no KEDA auto-install, no standalone HPA). No conditional branches are required. Docs-authoring epic present per Rule 11, consistent with the strategy's documentation support requirement and HLR-7.\n52\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-984475b99c625cd7","agent_id":"ac4122934639fa38c","session_id":"207785e0-7a4d-4b13-b5f6-b50ef683c695","uuid":"899aeecb-21f5-4ecd-b172-07bebee1e3bf","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:58:16.614Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-893d2430767ab1cf","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8008 decomposition"}
