{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0c29927655acfe2","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8008\n3\ttitle: Inference-Aware Autoscaling for llm-d- KEDA primitives\n4\tstatus: Refinement\n5\tpriority: Critical\n6\tlabels:\n7\t- refined\n8\tlinks:\n9\t- 'source-rfe: RHAIRFE-7770'\n10\t- 'related-strat: RHAISTRAT-7670'\n11\t- 'related-strat: RHAISTRAT-7397'\n12\t- 'related-strat: RHAISTRAT-7437'\n13\t- 'related-strat: RHAISTRAT-7599'\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## Business Need (from RFE)\n17\t\n18\t> The full business need is maintained in the source RFE: RHAIRFE-7770\n19\t\n20\t## Strategy (AI Generated by Agentic SDLC Pipeline)\n21\t\n22\t### TL;DR\n23\t\n24\tDeliver standalone KEDA autoscaling as a first-class path for LLMInferenceService alongside WVA-mediated autoscaling, following the upstream design in kserve/kserve#5646. Users configure KEDA triggers directly in the LLMInferenceService spec without deploying WVA, bringing feature parity with v1beta1 InferenceService. Both paths support scale-to/from-zero, independent prefill/decode scaling, and scaling observability. Ship with decision-guide documentation covering WVA-mediated autoscaling, standalone KEDA, and static allocation.\n25\t\n26\t**Components**: KServe (llmisvc-controller), odh-model-controller (KEDA auth context), Documentation\n27\t\n28\t### Technical Approach\n29\t\n30\t#### Two Autoscaling Paths: WVA-Mediated (Remains TP, RHAISTRAT-7670) and Standalone KEDA\n31\t\n32\tLLMInferenceService currently requires WVA when autoscaling is configured -- a CEL validation rule enforces `has(self.wva)` on the ScalingSpec. This forces all autoscaling users to deploy WVA even when they have existing KEDA infrastructure and want custom scaling triggers (CPU utilization, custom Prometheus queries, external queue depth). The RFE's example scenario -- a coding assistant deployment that scales prefill and decode pools during business hours and releases GPUs overnight -- can be served by either inference-aware WVA signals or simpler trigger-based scaling depending on the operator's requirements. The upstream design in kserve/kserve#5646 addresses this by introducing standalone KEDA as a first-class alternative, bringing LLMInferenceService to parity with v1beta1 InferenceService which already supports standalone KEDA with user-defined triggers.\n33\t\n34\tThe two paths are mutually exclusive, configured through `spec.scaling`: the WVA path uses WVA's analyzer engines (Saturation V2, Queueing Model, Throughput) to compute optimal replica counts published as `wva_desired_replicas` for HPA/KEDA to read (architecture context: workload-variant-autoscaler.md). WVA never directly modifies replicas -- actuation is delegated to HPA/KEDA, preventing conflicts between scaling controllers. The standalone KEDA path creates ScaledObjects with user-provided triggers, skipping VariantAutoscaling CR creation entirely. At least one path must be present when `spec.scaling` is configured, which remains mutually exclusive with static `spec.replicas`.\n35\t\n36\t#### API, Validation, and Controller Changes\n37\t\n38\tFollowing the upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839, the standalone KEDA path requires changes across three areas of the llmisvc-controller. API type changes in `pkg/apis/serving/v1alpha2/llm_inference_service_types.go` relax the CEL validation from requiring `wva` to requiring at least one of `wva` or `keda`, with mutual exclusivity enforced. A new standalone KEDA spec at the ScalingSpec level contains `pollingInterval`, `cooldownPeriod`, and a `triggers` field for user-defined KEDA trigger definitions (type, metadata map).\n39\t\n40\tValidation changes in `pkg/apis/serving/v1alpha2/llm_inference_service_validation.go` enforce mutual exclusivity and validate trigger completeness. For disaggregated deployments, both prefill and decode phases must use the same scaling path to maintain actuator consistency (architecture context: overlay 0011 -- actuator consistency constraint on the v1alpha2 validation webhook).\n41\t\n42\tController changes in `pkg/controller/v1alpha2/llmisvc/scaling.go` branch on which path is configured: standalone KEDA creates a ScaledObject using user-provided triggers and skips VariantAutoscaling CR creation. The v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA reconciler at `pkg/controller/v1beta1/inferenceservice/reconcilers/keda/keda_reconciler.go` provides the reference implementation for trigger construction supporting Resource, External, and PodMetric trigger types.\n43\t\n44\t#### Downstream Authentication for KEDA Prometheus Triggers\n45\t\n46\tOn OpenShift, KEDA ScaledObjects cannot query Thanos/Prometheus without an authenticated context. The existing odh-model-controller `kserve_keda_reconciler` (at `internal/controller/serving/reconcilers/kserve_keda_reconciler.go`) already creates per-namespace authentication resources for v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA autoscaling: a dedicated ServiceAccount (`inference-prometheus-auth`), a ServiceAccountToken Secret, a Role/RoleBinding for metrics access, and a KEDA TriggerAuthentication CR supplying bearer token and CA cert to KEDA's Prometheus triggers (architecture context: odh-model-controller.md -- `inference-prometheus-auth` secret is `kubernetes.io/service-account-token` type).\n47\t\n48\tFor LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA with Prometheus-type triggers, a parallel reconciler flow in odh-model-controller must create the same authentication context. The existing reconciler serves as the reference implementation for resource naming, owner reference management, and lifecycle cleanup. Documentation must instruct operators to reference the TriggerAuthentication resource in their KEDA trigger definitions for authenticated Prometheus/Thanos queries on OpenShift.\n49\t\n50\t#### Scale-to-Zero, Scale-from-Zero, and Disaggregated Scaling\n51\t\n52\tScale-to-zero works identically in both paths via KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0`, releasing GPU capacity when deployments are idle. In disaggregated deployments, all variants scale down together via model-level enforcement to prevent partial shutdown (architecture context: workload-variant-autoscaler.md -- scale-to-zero enforcer). Scale-to-zero requires KEDA as the actuator backend; HPA does not support scaling below `minReplicas: 1`.\n53\t\n54\tScale-from-zero differs by path: WVA's dedicated scale-from-zero engine monitors EPP flow control queue metrics and emits `wva_desired_replicas=1` when the EPP queues a request for an idle model (architecture context: workload-variant-autoscaler.md). In the standalone KEDA path, scale-from-zero depends on user-configured triggers (e.g., Prometheus query on EPP queue metrics). The request waits at the EPP until a pod reaches serving state. For disaggregated deployments, both paths support independent scaling per phase via phase-specific ScaledObjects or VariantAutoscaling CRs.\n55\t\n56\t### Affected Components\n57\t\n58\t* **KServe (llmisvc-controller)** (Serving team): API type changes (standalone KEDA spec in ScalingSpec, CEL validation update), scaling controller branching (standalone ScaledObject creation vs. WVA path), scaling status conditions for both paths\n59\t* **odh-model-controller** (Serving team): KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService standalone KEDA Prometheus triggers on OpenShift, following existing `kserve_keda_reconciler` pattern\n60\t* **Documentation** (Docs team): Autoscaling guides covering both paths, decision guide (WVA vs. standalone KEDA vs. static), troubleshooting, supported configuration matrix\n61\t\n62\t### Impacted Teams\n63\t\n64\t* **Serving team** (KServe llmisvc-controller): API and controller changes for standalone KEDA path (kserve/kserve#5646), autoscaling validation, scaling status conditions\n65\t* **Serving team** (odh-model-controller): Downstream KEDA TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService Prometheus triggers\n66\t* **Documentation team** (RHOAI docs): Autoscaling decision guide, configuration recipes, troubleshooting paths\n67\t\n68\t### High Level Requirements\n69\t\n70\t* \\[P0\\] LLMInferenceService supports standalone KEDA autoscaling with user-defined triggers without requiring WVA deployment (kserve/kserve#5646)\n71\t* \\[P0\\] The two autoscaling paths (WVA and standalone KEDA) are mutually exclusive at the API level with CEL validation enforcing at-least-one and at-most-one\n72\t* \\[P0\\] Scale-to-zero: idle deployments scale to zero replicas via KEDA `idleReplicaCount: 0` to release GPU capacity (both paths)\n73\t* \\[P0\\] Scale-from-zero: deployments at zero replicas scale up to serve incoming requests (WVA via EPP queue metrics; standalone KEDA via user-configured triggers)\n74\t* \\[P0\\] Disaggregated prefill and decode pools scale independently based on their respective load profiles (both paths)\n75\t* \\[P1\\] Scaling events visible to operators with signal attribution (KEDA ScaledObject events for standalone path; WVA events for WVA path)\n76\t* \\[P1\\] Well-lit path documentation covers both autoscaling paths with decision guide, configuration recipes per workload shape, and troubleshooting\n77\t\n78\t### Dependencies\n79\t\n80\t* **KEDA (Custom Metrics Autoscaler)** (external, exists): Required for standalone KEDA path and scale-to-zero; RHOAI does not auto-install KEDA (overlay 0008) -- documented as prerequisite with installation guidance referencing the GitOps repository (ODH-ADR-0007)\n81\t* **Prometheus / User Workload Monitoring** (external, exists): Required for KEDA metrics pipeline and as the primary supported standalone KEDA trigger source for inference metrics\n82\t* **EPP flow control metrics** (internal, exists): EPP exposes flow control queue size and dispatch rate metrics consumed by WVA's scale-from-zero engine and usable as standalone KEDA trigger source\n83\t* **Upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839** (external, in progress): Implements standalone KEDA support for LLMInferenceService upstream; if delayed, RHOAI can carry the patch downstream\n84\t* **v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA reconciler** (internal, exists): Reference implementation for standalone KEDA trigger construction at `pkg/controller/v1beta1/inferenceservice/reconcilers/keda/keda_reconciler.go`\n85\t\n86\t### Non-Functional Requirements\n87\t\n88\t* **Backwards Compatibility**: Static deployments with no autoscaling configured must work identically; existing WVA-mediated autoscaling must not change when standalone KEDA is available. The standalone KEDA path is additive -- existing LLMInferenceService CRs with `spec.scaling.wva` continue unchanged (RFE: Regression section).\n89\t* **Observability**: Scaling decision events emitted via KEDA ScaledObject events for standalone path. Scaling metrics exposed through KEDA ScaledObject metrics for dashboard integration (RFE: Observability section).\n90\t* **Security**: Prometheus/Thanos queries from KEDA use ServiceAccount token with TLS 1.2+ via TriggerAuthentication CR (architecture context: odh-model-controller.md). WVA metrics endpoint uses bearer token authentication via controller-runtime WithAuthenticationAndAuthorization filter (architecture context: workload-variant-autoscaler.md). No new authentication surfaces introduced.\n91\t* **Scalability**: Standalone KEDA path scalability depends on KEDA operator capacity (documented by KEDA project). No RHOAI-specific target for number of concurrent ScaledObjects specified in RFE or architecture context -- flagged in Open Questions.\n92\t\n93\t### Out-of-Scope\n94\t\n95\t* Multi-cluster autoscaling and fleet-level orchestration above llm-d (RFE scope boundary)\n96\t* Reducing model startup / cold start time -- separate RFEs: RHAIRFE-7753, RHAIRFE-7599, RHAIRFE-7139\n97\t* Tenant-level scaling policy and isolation (RHAIRFE-7333)\n98\t* Auto-installation of KEDA or other external operator dependencies (platform policy overlay 0008)\n99\t* Standalone HPA without WVA -- kserve/kserve#5646 identifies this as a potential future follow-up; HPA is supported only as an actuator within the WVA path\n100\t\n101\t### Acceptance Criteria (Proposed -- requires PM/Engineering validation)\n102\t\n103\t1. Given an LLMInferenceService with `spec.scaling.keda` configured with user-defined triggers and no WVA deployed, when the trigger condition is met (e.g., Prometheus query threshold exceeded), then the deployment scales up, measured by KEDA ScaledObject status showing active and Deployment replica count increasing.\n104\t\n105\t1. Given an LLMInferenceService with both `spec.scaling.wva` and `spec.scaling.keda` set, when the CR is submitted, then the API server rejects it with a CEL validation error indicating mutual exclusivity.\n106\t\n107\t1. Given an LLMInferenceService with `spec.scaling.keda` and no `spec.scaling.wva`, when the llmisvc-controller reconciles, then no VariantAutoscaling CR is created and a ScaledObject with user-provided triggers is created, measured by `kubectl get variantautoscalings` returning empty and `kubectl get scaledobjects` returning a ScaledObject with the user's triggers.\n108\t\n109\t1. Given an LLMInferenceService with KEDA and `idleReplicaCount: 0` (either path), when no requests are received for the cooldown period, then the deployment scales to zero, measured by Deployment replica count reaching 0 and KEDA ScaledObject status showing idle.\n110\t\n111\t1. Given a disaggregated LLMInferenceService with separate scaling configs (either path), when prefill pods reach saturation while decode pods remain below threshold, then only the prefill pool scales up, measured by independent scaling resource status for prefill and decode roles.\n112\t\n113\t1. Given a deployment with autoscaling active (either path), when Prometheus metrics become unavailable, then the deployment holds its current replica count, measured by replica count remaining stable during the metric outage.\n114\t\n115\t1. Static deployments with no autoscaling configured continue to serve identically, measured by no VariantAutoscaling, HPA, or ScaledObject resources created for deployments without `spec.scaling`.\n116\t\n117\t### Effort Estimate\n118\t\n119\t**M (Medium)** -- 2-3 sprints. KServe API and controller changes for standalone KEDA path (1-2 sprints -- API types, CEL validation, controller branching, status conditions; upstream PR #5839 provides reference). odh-model-controller TriggerAuthentication reconciler for LLMInferenceService (0.5 sprint, follows existing kserve_keda_reconciler pattern). Integration testing across standalone KEDA x 2 topologies (0.5 sprint, parallel). Documentation covering both paths with decision guide (0.5 sprint, parallel). Cross-team coordination: KServe team, ODH Platform team, Documentation team.\n120\t\n121\t### Risks\n122\t\n123\t* **Upstream PR acceptance timeline**: Standalone KEDA depends on kserve/kserve#5839 landing upstream. If delayed, timeline slips -- _Mitigation_: The PR is linked to accepted design issue #5646; RHOAI can carry the patch downstream with backport once merged.\n124\t* **Standalone KEDA scale-from-zero reliability**: Unlike WVA's dedicated scale-from-zero engine, standalone KEDA relies on user-configured triggers. Misconfigured triggers may fail the zero-to-one transition, leaving requests waiting indefinitely -- _Mitigation_: Document recommended trigger configurations for scale-from-zero using EPP queue metrics; provide example ScaledObject templates.\n125\t* **Scale-from-zero latency**: Time from first request to serving depends on model size, storage backend, and GPU initialization -- _Mitigation_: Document realistic cold start times; reference fast model startup RFEs (RHAIRFE-7753, RHAIRFE-7599, RHAIRFE-7139); recommend headroom configuration for latency-sensitive workloads.\n126\t* **KEDA dependency**: Both scale-to-zero and standalone KEDA require KEDA, which RHOAI does not auto-install (overlay 0008) -- _Mitigation_: Document KEDA as prerequisite with installation guidance referencing the GitOps repository (ODH-ADR-0007).\n127\t* **API surface complexity**: Two scaling paths in a single CRD may confuse operators -- _Mitigation_: Decision guide documentation with use-case-based recommendations; CEL validation provides clear error messages for invalid combinations.\n128\t\n129\t### Assumptions\n130\t\n131\t* The standalone KEDA API design from kserve/kserve#5646 is accepted upstream and PR #5839 lands -- needs validation (PR is open, assigned)\n132\t* KEDA ScaledObject with `idleReplicaCount: 0` correctly pauses and resumes scaling for LLM inference workloads -- needs validation\n133\t* EPP flow control queue metrics are reliably available when pods are at zero replicas (EPP itself must remain running) -- needs validation\n134\t* LLMInferenceService v1alpha2 autoscaling API fields (`spec.scaling`) are stable for the standalone KEDA addition -- confirmed (overlay 0011)\n135\t* The v1beta1 InferenceService KEDA reconciler provides a proven reference implementation for standalone KEDA trigger construction -- confirmed (architecture context: kserve.md)\n136\t\n137\t### Open Questions\n138\t\n139\t* **Does the upstream PR kserve/kserve#5839 require downstream adaptation beyond authentication context?** _(Owner: KServe team)_ -- The PR implements standalone KEDA upstream. RHOAI needs the odh-model-controller TriggerAuthentication flow plus potential RBAC and TLS adjustments for OpenShift.\n140\t* **What is the target p50/p95 for scale-from-zero time (trigger to serving)?** _(Owner: llm-d team)_ -- The RFE telemetry section references a 60-second p50 threshold. This determines headroom pre-scaling recommendations. Related: RHAISTRAT-7599 (Model Express), RHAISTRAT-7397 (Fast model weight loading), RHAISTRAT-7437 (Snapshot Restore).\n141\t* **What Prometheus capacity guidance should documentation include for fleet-scale deployments?** _(Owner: Performance/PSAP team)_ -- KEDA polls metrics at configurable intervals. At scale (e.g., Halvanor Digital's 2000-GPU fleet), this could impact Prometheus load.\n142\t\n143\t### Supporting Documentation\n144\t\n145\t* Source RFE: RHAIRFE-7770\n146\t* Upstream design: [kserve/kserve#5646](https://github.com/kserve/kserve/issues/5646) -- Standalone KEDA Scaling on LLMInferenceService\n147\t* Upstream implementation: [kserve/kserve#5839](https://github.com/kserve/kserve/pull/5839) -- PR implementing standalone KEDA support\n148\t* Architecture context: PLATFORM.md, workload-variant-autoscaler.md, kserve.md, llm-d.md, llm-d-inference-scheduler.md, odh-model-controller.md (RHOAI 3.5-ea.2)\n149\t* Architecture overlay: 0011 (KServe LLMInferenceService and llm-d integration architecture)\n150\t* Architecture overlay: 0008 (Platform policy -- RHOAI does not auto-install external operator dependencies)\n151\t* Related RFEs: RHAIRFE-7865 (WVA request-based autoscaling), RHAIRFE-7523 (scale-to-zero), RHAIRFE-7064 (custom metrics), RHAIRFE-7091 (autoscaling presets)\n152\t* Related STRATs: RHAISTRAT-7599 (Model Express), RHAISTRAT-7397 (Fast model weight loading), RHAISTRAT-7437 (Snapshot Restore)\n153\t* Design doc: [kserve/kserve#5646](https://github.com/kserve/kserve/issues/5646)\n154\t* [KEDA AutoScaling Recommendations]([internal link removed]) (for isvc)\n155\t\n156\t### Prerequisites & Process Gates\n157\t\n158\t* **Architecture Review**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n159\t* **ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n160\t* **Licence Validation**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n161\t* **Accelerator/Package Support**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n162\t* **Documentation Support**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n163\t* **UXD Support**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n164\t* **Performance Team Support**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n165\t\n166\t## Staff Engineer / SME Input\n167\t\n168\t_Add technical corrections, architectural direction, component preferences, or domain expertise below. Write in declarative, cumulative form -- statements that remain valid across refinement iterations. This input takes priority over architecture context when they conflict. After review: address findings, then remove the needs-attention label from Jira._\n169\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f5be360b42a61fc","agent_id":"a074c867882eb1171","session_id":"207785e0-7a4d-4b13-b5f6-b50ef683c695","uuid":"84d0dbc0-1e9e-4031-9130-cbabd11c8154","timestamp":"2026-10-22T07:59:29.996Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a0c29927655acfe2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8008 decomposition"}
