{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9813d0cbafdd5fd0","type":"tool_result","content":"16:**Detailed**: odh-model-controller runs as two Deployments within the RHOAI platform. The primary `odh-model-controller` Deployment is a controller-runtime operator that reconciles KServe InferenceService, InferenceGraph, LLMInferenceService, ServingRuntime, and NIM Account custom resources. It manages subordinate Kubernetes resources including Routes, NetworkPolicies, ServiceAccounts, ClusterRoleBindings, KEDA TriggerAuthentications, Kuadrant AuthPolicies, Istio EnvoyFilters, and Prometheus monitoring resources. Eight admission webhooks (5 mutating, 3 validating) intercept creation and update of inference workloads and NIM accounts. The secondary `model-serving-api` Deployment provides an HTTPS REST API for gateway discovery (`/api/v1/gateways`) and sample LLM-d templates (`/api/v1/samples/llm-d`), backed by a Kubernetes client that queries Gateway API resources. Both workloads resolve their TLS configuration from the OpenShift APIServer TLS profile, falling back to Mozilla Intermediate defaults (TLS 1.2+) on non-OpenShift clusters. The controller watches the OpenShift `config.openshift.io/v1/APIServer` resource and restarts on TLS profile changes. [source: cmd/main.go:89-177, server/main.go:27-134, server/server.go:16-44, pkg/tls/tls.go:76-139]\n22:**Controller architecture.** The operator runs 10 reconcilers spanning core Kubernetes resources (ConfigMap, Secret, Pod), KServe serving resources (InferenceService, InferenceGraph, ServingRuntime), LLM-specific resources (LLMInferenceService, Gateway), NIM account management, and OpenShift APIServer TLS profile watching. The InferenceServiceReconciler is the most broadly connected, owning subordinate watches on 13 resource types including Routes, NetworkPolicies, ServiceAccounts, ClusterRoleBindings, PodMonitors, ServiceMonitors, ServingRuntimes, and KEDA TriggerAuthentications. The GatewayReconciler manages Gateway API resources, Kuadrant AuthPolicies, Istio EnvoyFilters, and PodMonitors for the LLM gateway ingress path. A conditional prometheus-operator watch allows the controller to degrade gracefully when monitoring CRDs are absent. [source: cmd/main.go:89-177, internal/controller/serving/inferenceservice_controller.go:208-219, internal/controller/serving/llm/gateway_controller.go:1003-1062]\n24:**API server architecture.** The model-serving-api Deployment provides a TLS-mandatory REST API. The `/api/v1/gateways` endpoint requires Bearer token authentication via a middleware that extracts tokens from the Authorization header and returns 401 for unauthenticated requests. The `/api/v1/samples/llm-d` endpoint is intentionally unauthenticated, serving static embedded YAML templates. A dedicated metrics listener on port 8080 serves Prometheus metrics over HTTPS without application-level authentication. OpenTelemetry tracing is optionally exported via OTLP/gRPC when configured. [source: server/server.go:16-44, server/middleware/auth.go:24-41, server/main.go:91-98, server/observability/observability.go:67-97]\n28:**Platform integration pattern.** The controller reads `DataScienceCluster` and `DSCInitialization` CRs to determine which platform components are enabled, gating feature-specific behavior. It manages Kuadrant AuthPolicies for LLMInferenceService authentication, creates KEDA TriggerAuthentications for autoscaling, and provisions OpenShift Routes for model serving endpoints. The SelfSubjectAccessReview call in the gateway discoverer validates the caller's permissions before exposing gateway information. [source: config/rbac/role.yaml:3, server/gateway/discovery.go:154-171]\n117:| github.com/kedacore/keda/v2 | v2.18.3 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n129:| github.com/prometheus-operator/prometheus-operator/pkg/apis/monitoring | v0.89.0 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n130:| github.com/prometheus/client_golang | v1.23.2 | Yes | runtime-observability | runtime-observability |\n135:| go.opentelemetry.io/otel/exporters/prometheus | v0.63.0 | Yes |  | Go module dependency |\n165:| prometheus-operator | CRD CRUD | unknown | Manage Prometheus monitoring resources |\n169:| prometheus-operator | Controller watch | runtime-integration | Manage Prometheus monitoring resources |\n170:| prometheus-operator | Controller watch (conditional) | runtime-integration | Manage Prometheus monitoring resources |\n201:| kserve-prometheus-k8s |  | endpoints, pods, services | get, list, watch |\n213:| odh-model-controller-role | keda.sh | triggerauthentications | create, delete, get, list, patch, update, watch |\n338:The InferenceServiceReconciler watches InferenceService, ServingRuntime, ConfigMap, Secret, and Namespace resources. On reconciliation, it creates or updates subordinate resources including Services, ServiceAccounts, NetworkPolicies, ClusterRoleBindings, Routes, PodMonitors, ServiceMonitors, and KEDA TriggerAuthentications. [source: internal/controller/serving/inferenceservice_controller.go:208-219, config/rbac/role.yaml:3]\n388:| /v1/Secret | Resource CRUD |  |  |  | Unknown | create, delete, get, list, patch, update operations by APIKeyManager, ConfigMapReconciler, KserveKEDAReconciler, PodReconciler, SecretReconciler, ServingRuntimeReconciler, ValidationHandler, secretHandler |\n392:| /v1/ServiceAccount | Resource CRUD |  |  |  | Unknown | create, delete, get operations by KserveKEDAReconciler, ServiceAccountReconciler, serviceAccountHandler |\n419:| keda.sh/v1alpha1/TriggerAuthentication | Controller watch (Owns) |  |  | Kubernetes API | TLS | InferenceServiceReconciler |\n426:| prometheus-operator | CRD CRUD | unknown |  | HTTPS | TLS 1.2+ | Manage Prometheus monitoring resources |\n427:| prometheus-operator | Controller watch |  |  |  | Unknown | Manage Prometheus monitoring resources |\n428:| prometheus-operator | Controller watch (conditional) |  |  |  | Unknown | Manage Prometheus monitoring resources |\n---\n1:# Component: workload-variant-autoscaler\n5:- **Repository**: https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/workload-variant-autoscaler.git\n14:**Short**: Kubernetes controller that performs intelligent autoscaling for inference model servers based on saturation metrics collected from Prometheus, with support for HPA, KEDA ScaledObject, InferencePool, and LeaderWorkerSet workloads. [source: cmd/main.go:446, 664, Dockerfile.konflux:34, config/base/rbac/manager-clusterrole.yaml:2]\n16:**Detailed**: workload-variant-autoscaler is a controller-runtime operator that watches HorizontalPodAutoscaler, InferencePool (via gateway-api-inference-extension), ScaledObject (via KEDA), and ConfigMap resources to drive autoscaling decisions for inference workloads. It queries Prometheus for real-time metrics—connecting over HTTPS with TLS 1.2+ minimum, optional mTLS via client certificates, and bearer token authentication—and uses optimization engines including Kalman filter-based prediction to determine scaling actions. The controller manages Deployment and LeaderWorkerSet scale subresources directly, discovers GPU-equipped nodes via the Kubernetes API, and supports a GPU rebalance plugin that inspects ResourceQuota objects. Configuration is dynamically loaded from ConfigMaps, with a dedicated ConfigMap reconciler that bootstraps initial state and propagates runtime changes. The metrics endpoint on port 8443 is protected by controller-runtime's TokenReview and SubjectAccessReview filter, while health and readiness probes on port 8081 are unauthenticated. The component builds with `GOEXPERIMENT=strictfipsruntime` and `CGO_ENABLED=1` for FIPS-compliant dynamic linking on a UBI9 base image. [source: cmd/main.go:272, 446, 664, 668, Dockerfile.konflux:9, 19, internal/prometheus/tls.go:48-53, internal/prometheus/prometheus_transport.go:38-72]\n20:The workload-variant-autoscaler follows a multi-loop controller pattern built on controller-runtime, with four independent reconciliation loops—ConfigMap, HPA, InferencePool, and ScaledObject—each watching a distinct Kubernetes resource type and feeding state into a shared datastore. This design decouples discovery of autoscaling targets from the optimization engine that computes scaling decisions, allowing the controller to support heterogeneous workload types (Deployments, LeaderWorkerSets, KEDA ScaledObjects, and Gateway API InferencePools) through a unified scaling path. [source: cmd/main.go:272, 446, internal/controller/scaledobject_reconciler.go:67-72]\n24:The KEDA integration is conditional: the ScaledObject controller watches resources only when KEDA CRDs are present and uses annotation-based predicates to filter for managed objects. Similarly, InferencePool reconciliation depends on gateway-api-inference-extension types being available in the cluster. This conditional design makes both dependencies optional at runtime, which is significant for RHOAI deployments where not all clusters will have KEDA or the Gateway API inference extension installed. [source: internal/controller/scaledobject_reconciler.go:67-72, config/base/rbac/manager-clusterrole.yaml:2]\n34:| Upstream | https://github.com/opendatahub-io/workload-variant-autoscaler | auto_merge | main | -- | sync_config |\n35:| Downstream | https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/workload-variant-autoscaler | auto_merge | main | `sync-manifests-to-kserve.yml` | local_analysis |\n49:| HorizontalPodAutoscaler controller | Controller | Reconciles HorizontalPodAutoscaler resources |\n51:| ScaledObject controller | Controller | Reconciles ScaledObject resources |\n119:| KEDA | Controller watch (conditional) | runtime-integration | Optional ScaledObject discovery for autoscaling targets |\n153:| manager-role | apps | deployments/scale | get, update |\n155:| manager-role | autoscaling | horizontalpodautoscalers | get, list, patch, update, watch |\n157:| manager-role | keda.sh | scaledobjects | get, list, patch, update, watch |\n159:| manager-role | leaderworkerset.x-k8s.io | leaderworkersets/scale | get, update |\n172:| metrics-reader-rolebinding | workload-variant-autoscaler-monitoring | metrics-reader (ClusterRole) | kube-prometheus-stack-prometheus |\n219:- **Kubernetes control plane interaction:** Four reconciliation loops watch ConfigMap, HPA, InferencePool, and ScaledObject resources via controller-runtime informers (TLS-secured Kubernetes API). Scaling actions are applied by updating `autoscaling/v1/Scale` subresources on Deployments and LeaderWorkerSets. GPU topology is discovered by listing Node resources. All Kubernetes API access uses the controller-manager ServiceAccount with RBAC enforced via the manager-role ClusterRole. [source: cmd/main.go:272, config/base/rbac/manager-clusterrole.yaml:2, config/base/manager/deployment.yaml:1]\n240:| KEDA | Controller watch (conditional) |  |  |  | Unknown | Optional ScaledObject discovery for autoscaling targets |\n245:| autoscaling/v1/Scale | Resource CRUD |  |  |  | Unknown | update operations by DirectActuator |\n246:| autoscaling/v2/HorizontalPodAutoscaler | Controller watch (For) |  |  | Kubernetes API | TLS | HPAReconciler |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f5be360b42a61fc","agent_id":"a074c867882eb1171","session_id":"207785e0-7a4d-4b13-b5f6-b50ef683c695","uuid":"abd6e2aa-d9d6-4a56-bd6e-e491027e009e","timestamp":"2026-10-22T08:01:55.127Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9813d0cbafdd5fd0","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8008 decomposition"}
